# G016 — Reverse a String

*v2. See `reverse-string.md` for prose: first content-level operation
(vs value-level), character vs word granularity as first-class, text
natives are infrastructure because agents communicate in text.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Content Pipeline Across Granularities

[content-pipeline @document
    IN ORDER TO
        let three agents transform @document at their preferred granularity
        then verify meaning is preserved across the combined output

    concurrent [
        vincent: @vincent{apply stylistic reversal where beneficial
                          on: @document}
    |
        sylvia:  @sylvia{restructure sentences for clarity
                         on: @document}
    |
        lena:    @lena{summarise each section at paragraph level
                       of: @document}
    ]
    join

    <- @eliana{verify transformations preserve meaning}
        where each agent operated at its own granularity
        and the composed output remains coherent across levels
]

---

## Verb Surface

[reverse-string
    {s}                                     ;; character-level
]

[reverse-words
    {s}                                      ;; word-level
]

[palindrome?
    {s}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: granularity selects the unit `where` meaning depends

[text-op {s: @s, level: @lvl}]
    IN ORDER TO
        apply the same transformation at different semantic units
        because character-reversal and word-reversal are both valid
        but produce different meanings from the same input

    @lvl
        where "char"      -> operate on codepoints
        where "word"      -> split on whitespace, operate on tokens
        where "sentence"  -> split on terminators, operate on sentences
]

The resolver must know which level you mean. Same content, different
structural level, different result — the granularity parameter is
as load-bearing as the operation itself.

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [reverse-string {s: "Hello, World!"}]    ;; -> "!dlroW ,olleH"
    -> [reverse-words  {s: "Hello, World!"}]    ;; -> "World! Hello,"
    -> [palindrome?    {s: "racecar"}]          ;; -> true
]
